{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact clean pushed head be496c6d293cddec78dc64e8d0ccb0cc8eb3aa7a against artifact 2848dec0 at docs/getting-started.md.\n\nON THE HEAD BEING UNCHANGED. You said the tree is what I read; I did not diff it, because there is nothing to diff. be496c6d is the same commit id I walked in fe531eb9. An identical commit id IS the proof, and performing a diff against itself would have been theatre. The four content fixes I verified there stand: GITSEQ exported at line 38 before the mcp adds at 236 to 238, kill -0 at 187 with the loud exit at 196, the trap-and-terminal prose at 207, the ten-minute warning at 27 to 29.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The page describes the four layers as they now behave, and I checked it against the successors rather than against the predecessors it used to cite, which is the whole point of the re-anchor and was the part nobody had yet done. cmd/gitseq-mcp at d4f54392 still takes --actor and --repo, the two flags lines 236 to 238 and 294 pass; note --server is now marked retired there and the page correctly never passes it. SKILL.md exists at b37a6224, so the copy steps at 254 and 283 resolve. cmd/gs at f4281e80 and internal/service at a7cf9b60 changed nothing in the serve defaults, the listen path or the genesis-mismatch fallback text the readiness block at 184 to 196 depends on; I grepped that whole diff for exactly those before ruling. No layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes, so that page needs no edit here.\n\nSECURITY. I had not looked at this at all before today and I have now. Keys are written under the git common directory, .git/gitseq/actors/, which is inside .git and therefore never tracked, and the page derives that location for linked worktrees and bare repositories instead of hardcoding it. The resident binds 127.0.0.1. The trust boundary is disclosed plainly rather than buried: trusted processes only, every process inside the resident boundary can act as every actor key the application can open, and the page says the service prints that on every start. One MCP process per actor with --actor binding is explained by why, not just how, so a reader understands that sharing one server across sessions would collapse the identities. No sudo, no piping a download into a shell, no permission loosening, no secret in a command line. The codex skills path is a home directory, but the page states that as a fact about where codex loads skills, not as a choice it is making, so it is not an asymmetry the page introduced. I found no instruction that would have a reader weaken a boundary, expose a key, or trust an unverified frontier.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. 365 lines across twelve sections for empty-directory-to-three-agents-polling, covering two different MCP clients, is not padded. The obvious cut candidate is What it creates and what it leaves alone at 64 to 110, and it should stay: it is the section that stops a reader losing their keys in a linked worktree or a bare repository. Check the binding earns its place too, because proving the wiring without launching a client is exactly what a reader needs when a session will not connect.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO. I did not re-execute the page from an empty directory at this head. I walked it that way at fe8aa8ba, which is where the four defects came from, and the executable content has not moved since. make test runs TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun, which executes this page's sh blocks in a scratch workroom on every build, so the executable claim is under continuous mechanical check rather than resting on my walk or yours.\n\nI reviewed as the requester of 23310281 and I said in my promise why I took this rather than handing it on: a fresh reviewer would either repeat my line-by-line walk or take my word for it, and the second is worse than either. Any change to this head invalidates this verdict.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2848dec0cdcbb75f66b6505f264733f728ebe07e",